lier with no correlation identifier, so it cannot establish two independent sources or discriminate either mechanism. The lone backend metric, incident_1003-E-001 (request_latency_ms=40 ms at 12:00:00Z), is a single low window-start sample that neither supports nor excludes local pressure, and cause_discriminator_ids is empty for both candidates while candidate_assessments reports the same unresolved questions, an unresolved competitor, and no verifier acceptance. The changes source was queried with no filters and no time bounds and returned zero records (query_changes completed, record_count 0), which covers the entire supplied pack; that empty result is a persisting causal gap but not proof of health, and repeating the same unbounded query would only re-request unavailable telemetry, so it is not listed as a blocking check. The two blocking gaps are therefore the unobserved backend-internal saturation telemetry via named metric scopes (the causal source for the selected candidate) and the unqueried postgres connection/active-session role, whose connection-wait or session build-up signatures would discriminate the selected candidate from the competing postgres_slow_query candidate. Payment logs and the repeat backend-log window are corroboration only and are placed in optional_checks. Because a sufficient verdict requires empty requested_checks and contradictions, the verdict is insufficient.\"}, {\"target_hypothesis_id\": \"hyp-32a63ad13f743388bf95b6fc388791fe4b2369f6ac15c6a4124f9cf3b94a479d\", \"verdict\": \"insufficient\", \"contradictions\": [\"The selected candidate is anchored only by incident_1003-E-003 (backend 'Request processing exceeded deadline' at 2026-10-01T12:00:14Z), while incident_1003-E-002 records the gateway logging 'Request completed with HTTP 200' at 2026-10-01T12:00:00Z; the two records name different services, are 14 seconds apart, and carry no request/correlation identifier or severity metadata, so they neither establish a common request pa